{
  "summary": "This experiment replaced average pooling with transformer (attention-based) aggregation in the RNA-only, multiprofile, scVI_n200 setting, constituting a single-variable change from llm_summary_006 to test whether learned inter-profile attention captures signal that simple averaging discards. The validation accuracy of 0.653 (F1 0.628) is essentially equivalent to the avgpool result (0.650, F1 0.628), with only a ~0.3% accuracy difference, suggesting that the aggregation strategy is not a major performance differentiator in the RNA-only multiprofile setting and that both strategies capture the multiprofile gain comparably. It remains uncertain whether the marginal difference reflects a genuine (if small) advantage of transformer aggregation or run-to-run variability, and whether transformer aggregation might interact differently with multimodal fusion, which was the original motivation for testing it given the underperformance of the multimodal multiprofile configuration (llm_summary_007)."
}

